  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}x(\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({e}^{3})^{2})\\,({e}^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})\\,(1)]-2{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6})-2x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =6{e}^{3}-2({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
